add(0,20,[mat('a','Evaluate the polynomial at the three nodes.',[[2],[6],[12]],[r'At \(-1,0,1\), \(x^2+5x+6\) gives \(2,6,12\), respectively.']),c('b','Choose the linearity argument.','Evaluation at each fixed node preserves every linear combination',['Evaluation preserves multiplication, therefore every evaluation map is nonlinear','All quadratic polynomials have the same evaluations'],[r'At each node t, \((\alpha p+\beta q)(t)=\alpha p(t)+\beta q(t)\).',r'Collecting the three evaluations gives \(T(\alpha p+\beta q)=\alpha T(p)+\beta T(q)\).']),c('c','Choose the argument that T is one-to-one.','A polynomial of degree at most 2 vanishing at −1,0,1 must be the zero polynomial',['Every quadratic has three distinct roots','The domain has more dimensions than the codomain'],[r'If \(T(p)=0\), p vanishes at the three distinct evaluation nodes.',r'A nonzero polynomial of degree at most 2 cannot have three distinct roots. Thus the kernel is zero.']),expr('d','Inverse image of (0,3,0)',r'3-3x^2',r'3x^2-3',r'3x',[r'Let \(p=a+bx+cx^2\). From \(p(0)=3\) obtain \(a=3\).',r'The equations \(3-b+c=0\) and \(3+b+c=0\) give \(b=0,c=-3\).']),c('e','Choose the graph description.','Downward-opening parabola with vertex (0,3), roots (−1,0) and (1,0), symmetric about the y-axis',['Upward-opening parabola with vertex (0,−3)','Line through (−1,0) and (1,0)'],[r'The formula \(y=3-3x^2\) has negative leading coefficient and vertex \((0,3)\).',r'Solve \(3-3x^2=0\) to get roots \(x=\pm1\). Plot these three points and draw the symmetric downward parabola.'])])
add(0,21,[c('a','Choose the linearity proof.','At every node xi, (αp+βq)(xi)=αp(xi)+βq(xi)',['At every node, p(xi)q(xi)=p(xi)+q(xi)','Linearity holds only for equally spaced nodes'],[r'Evaluation at each fixed point respects sums and scalar multiples.',r'Putting the three scalar identities into one vector identity proves T is linear.']),c('b','Choose the injectivity proof.','The zero-output polynomial has three distinct roots but degree at most 2, so it is identically zero',['Every polynomial of degree at most 2 has three roots','Distinct nodes force every polynomial to be constant'],[r'The strict inequalities ensure three distinct nodes.',r'If all three values vanish, the root bound forces the polynomial to be zero; hence the kernel is trivial.']),expr('c','Which evaluation identity verifies the proposed inverse?',r'P_i(x_j)=\delta_{ij}\quad\Rightarrow\quad T(\sum_{i=1}^3a_iP_i)=(a_1,a_2,a_3)',r'P_i(x_j)=1\text{ for every }i,j',r'P_i(x_j)=0\text{ for every }i,j',[r'The denominators in each \(P_i\) are nonzero because the nodes are distinct.',r'At its own node, numerator and denominator agree, giving 1. At another node a numerator factor vanishes, giving 0.',r'Thus \(T(a_1P_1+a_2P_2+a_3P_3)=(a_1,a_2,a_3)\). The sum has degree at most 2, and injectivity makes it the unique inverse image.']),c('d','How does the graph relate to the three points?','It passes through all three points (x1,a1),(x2,a2),(x3,a3)',['It passes through their midpoint only','The points are always its x-intercepts'],[r'At \(x=x_j\), all terms except \(a_jP_j(x_j)\) vanish, leaving \(a_j\).',r'Therefore the graph is the unique polynomial interpolant of degree at most 2 through the three specified points; it may be a line or constant in special cases.'])])
add(0,22,[c('a','Choose the mathematically valid statement about L as printed.','The formula is linear into the space of all functions, but it need not map the stated V into V',['It is always a linear operator V → V with the printed hypotheses','It fails additivity even as a map into all functions'],[r'Expanding derivatives gives \(L(\alpha y+\beta z)=\alpha(y\prime\prime+py\prime+qy)+\beta(z\prime\prime+pz\prime+qz)=\alpha Ly+\beta Lz\).',r'However, the printed codomain V consists of twice differentiable functions. With \(p=q=0\) and \(y=|x|^3\), the input is twice differentiable but \(Ly=6|x|\) is not differentiable at zero.',r'Thus the correct linearity claim uses a function-space codomain large enough to contain the outputs, for example all real functions on the line; the stated operator V → V is not generally well-defined.']),expr('b','Verify the claimed kernel elements in the special case.',r'L(c_1\sin x+c_2\cos x)=(-c_1\sin x-c_2\cos x)+(c_1\sin x+c_2\cos x)=0',r'L(c_1\sin x+c_2\cos x)=2c_1\sin x+2c_2\cos x',r'L(c_1\sin x+c_2\cos x)=c_1\cos x-c_2\sin x',[r'For \(p=0,q=1\), the formula becomes \(L\phi=\phi\prime\prime+\phi\).',r'Both sine and cosine have second derivative equal to their negative. The terms cancel for every pair of real coefficients, so all stated functions are in the kernel of the corrected map.'])],notice='The full source prints L:V→V with V consisting of twice differentiable functions. Its formula need not produce a twice differentiable output. Part (a) explicitly identifies this codomain defect and proves linearity into a valid larger function space; part (b) is unchanged.')
